rent discussion round: 1. Agent 1 just contributed. Now it's our turn (Agent 2). We need to respond to Agent 1's point, either agree, challenge, or add new info. We have our own candidate notes. We should bring in facts about other candidates, perhaps challenge that Candidate C is best, or add nuance.\n\nWe have notes: Candidate A can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. No info on reliability, stress, etc.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to discuss criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nAgent 1's argument: Candidate C has reliability, decision quality, positive crew atmosphere, but communication issues.\n\nWe can bring up Candidate B's strong stress handling and conscientiousness, but also note his nasty remarks (communication/crew cooperation). Or Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving but hot temper and know-it-all (crew cooperation). Or Candidate A's technical competence and leadership but show-off and not open to new ideas (crew cooperation, adaptability). We need to decide whether to keep recommending C or shift.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate B's stress resilience and conscientiousness are crucial for long-distance flights, and his technical skills (weather assessment, computer) are important, but his nasty remarks are a serious crew cooperation issue. Candidate C's communication difficulty may be more severe. We could suggest that Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving are strong, but hot temper is concerning.\n\nWe need to decide our current recommendation. We could either stay with Candidate C, or shift to another candidate. Let's evaluate: Candidate C has reliability and decision quality, but communication issues. Candidate B has stress resilience and conscientiousness, but communication issues (nasty remarks). Both have crew cooperation concerns. Candidate D has responsibility and problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all. Candidate A has technical competence and leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nWhich candidate overall best fits? Hard. Perhaps Candidate B's stress handling is critical for long-distance flights, and his conscientiousness indicates reliability. The nasty remarks are concerning but could be mitigated with training. Candidate C's communication difficulty may be more fundamental. So we might shift to Candidate B.\n\nWe need to provide evidence: B handles stress very well, very conscientious (reliability). Also B has weak memory for numbers (attention) and makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation). We can argue that while B has some concerns, his strengths in stress resilience and reliability outweigh C's communication issues.\n\nThus we can change vote to Candidate B.\n\nWe must reference Agent 1's point and provide evidence.\n\nPublic message: \"I see the strengths of C, but I think B's stress resilience and conscientiousness are more critical, and his communication issue is also a problem but perhaps less severe than C's difficulty communicating ideas.\" Provide evidence.\n\nLet's craft.\n\nMetadata vote: Candidate B."}
